),(z,x)}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416066 1071 "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R=\\{(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)\\}$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1071 "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)}$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416066 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)\\}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416067 1071 "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1071 "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416068 1071 "Let $A=\\{3,6,8\\}$ and $B=\\{1,4,7\\}$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\}?$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1071 "Let $A={3,6,8}$ and $B={1,4,7}$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)}?$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416069 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{7,8,9,10\\},\\,B=\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416069 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{7,8,9,10\\},\\,B=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416070 1071 "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81\\}?$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1071 "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81}?$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416071 1071 "Let $A=\\{Z,Q,R\\}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X\\}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1071 "Let $A={Z,Q,R}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186416072 1071 "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X=\\{2,4,6\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(4,4)$\n\nIII. $(6,2)$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1071 "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X={2,4,6}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(4,4)$\n\nIII. $(6,2)$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186419024 1071 "Suppose we have the set $A=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ Which of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times A\\,:\\,x<y\\}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186419024 1071 "Suppose we have the set $A={1,2,3,4}.$ Which of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in A\\times A\\,:\\,x<y}\n$$" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false] [17592186419024 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following set:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times A$ such that $x<y.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2,3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),\\,(1,3),$ and $(1,4),$ and we should add the arrows $1\\to 2,\\,1\\to 3,$ and $1\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,3)$ and $(2,4),$ and we should add the arrows $2\\to 3$ and $2\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,4),$ and we should add the arrow $3\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=4$ there are no values $y\\in A$ such that $x<y.$ Therefore we do not add arrows starting from $4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png)" 13194139534477 17592186045417 true] [17592186419024 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times A$ such that $x<y.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2,3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),\\,(1,3),$ and $(1,4),$ and we should add the arrows $1\\to 2,\\,1\\to 3,$ and $1\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,3)$ and $(2,4),$ and we should add the arrows $2\\to 3$ and $2\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,4),$ and we should add the arrow $3\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=4$ there are no values $y\\in A$ such that $x<y.$ Therefore we do not add arrows starting from $4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png)" 13194139534477 17592186045417 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
